<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :theme_slots %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:theme_slots) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Semantic channels') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The semantic channels are the preferred API: a few lines re-theme the whole screen, and every
            component that reads a channel follows. The channels are a fixed set:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>Backgrounds: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">canvas</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">surface</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">backdrop</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-strong</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-muted</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-soft</code></li>
            <li>Text: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-primary</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-secondary</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-inverse</code></li>
            <li>Stroke: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">stroke-contrast</code></li>
            <li>Borders: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-strong</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-muted</code></li>
            <li>Icon: one global channel for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> paint, defaulting to text-primary. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image) %>.</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-bg($semantic, $token)</code>: points a
              background channel at a token.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-text($semantic, $token)</code>: points a
              text channel at a token, carrying the full text paint (ink, tile, clip).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-stroke($semantic, $token)</code>: points
              the stroke channel at a token.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-border($semantic, $token)</code>: points a
              border channel at a token's fill paint.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-icon($token)</code>: optional paint for
              adaptive icons; omit it and icons follow text-primary.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every semantic mixin takes <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$raw: true</code>, which
            points the channel at a root palette variable and skips the mode pipeline. The framework uses it for its
            own unthemed dark defaults, where the dark remap would invert the value a second time. Theme files have
            no use for it; see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.semantic-bg("canvas", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-bg("surface", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-primary", "black");
@include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-secondary", "yellow-30");
@include theme-slots.semantic-stroke("stroke-contrast", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-border("border-muted", "yellow-30");',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Component slots') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Component slots tune one surface without moving a whole channel. A slot name is the variable fragment a
            component reads, and each slot takes one kind of paint. These are the slots components consume today:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>Background slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen-backdrop</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta-emphasis-2</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta-emphasis-3</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-track</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill-emphasis-2</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill-emphasis-3</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-dot</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-dot-current</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-meta</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-meta-device</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code></li>
            <li>Text slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label-gray</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code></li>
            <li>Border slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-head-row</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-body-row</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label-underline</code></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code> is a pair, one background slot and one
            text slot for the same surface: a solid marker box with readable text on it, for plugin surfaces the
            framework has no component for. It is the only slot whose default names semantic channels instead of a
            token, taking its field from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-strong</code> and
            its text from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-inverse</code>. State both halves
            in a theme when the marker box needs its own pairing, the way White and Red gives it a white field with
            red text.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Pointing a slot at a channel it does not take is silent. A background slot given
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-slot</code> writes four variables no component
            reads, and nothing on the screen changes.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg-slot($slot, $token)</code>: a slot's background
              paint.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-slot($slot, $token)</code>: a slot's text
              paint, including the tile and clip.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-level-slot($slot, $level, $dir: h)</code>:
              points a slot's border at one of the framework border levels.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-token-slot($slot, $token, $dir: h)</code>:
              points a slot's border at a token's fill paint instead of a level.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Slot values cascade from the screen, so you can set them again on a component or state wrapper.
            On unthemed screens the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">inverse</code> class supplies local defaults for the opposite of the screen's scheme.
            Inside a theme the subtree keeps the theme's slots, and the theme's inverse rule restates the ones that flip with the ground.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.bg-slot("title-bar", "yellow-40");
@include theme-slots.bg-slot("progress-fill", "yellow-55");
@include theme-slots.text-slot("label-gray", "yellow-30");
@include theme-slots.border-token-slot("label-underline", "yellow-30");

.screen--theme-example .inverse {
    @include theme-slots.bg-slot("progress-fill", "yellow-20");
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Utility remaps') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Utility remaps re-point the raw palette utilities
            (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--*</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text--*</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--*</code>), so plugin markup that names
            gray tokens follows the theme without edits.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-grayscale($to-hue, $side)</code>: the
              bulk remap of the whole grayscale ladder to a hue. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$side: 'dark'</code>
              (default) compresses grays into the hue's dark steps 10 to 40, keeps black as ink, and sends white to
              the hue; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'bright'</code> compresses into steps 45
              to 75, sends black to the hue, and keeps white; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'linear'</code>
              maps gray steps 1:1 to hue steps. Strokes are the exception: the token sent to the hue takes the
              nearest hue step for its stroke instead (45 under bright, 40 under dark, 75 under linear), so
              outlines never dissolve into the canvas.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-border-grayscale($to-hue, $side)</code>:
              the same idea for the two border line inks, recoloring the staggered patterns without touching their
              geometry.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-bg($token, $remap-to)</code> /
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-text($token, $remap-to)</code> /
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-stroke($token, $remap-to)</code>: remap one
              utility token, for exceptions on top of a bulk remap.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-token($level, $dir, $token)</code>:
              remap one border level and direction to a token's paint.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-level($level, $dir, $from-level)</code>:
              point one border level at another level's art, fields and render program included. This is the handle
              the 2-bit rail needs, where the four tones are literal in the line data and no token remap can reach
              them. Restating a level onto itself is not a no-op: it also pins that level to the family's own line
              polarity.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The three shipped themes show every side: Black and Yellow is a dark-side remap; White and Red is a
            bright-side remap plus one exception, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-bg("white", "red")</code>,
            so white surfaces adopt the canvas hue while white text stays ink. Dark mirrors the grayscale axis and
            leans on <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-level</code> for the 2-bit
            rail and for the one level that maps to itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '// White and Red: bright-side remap with one exception.
@include theme-slots.utility-remap-grayscale("red", $side: "bright");
@include theme-slots.utility-remap-border-grayscale("red", $side: "bright");
@include theme-slots.utility-bg("white", "red");',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Border levels and lines') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Border levels are the seven-step intensity scale the border pipeline renders: 1 is black, 2 through 6
            step through the grays (gray-15, gray-30, gray-40, gray-50, gray-65), 7 is white, each with an
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">h</code> and a
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">v</code> direction.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-level-slot</code> points a component slot at a
            level; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-token</code> re-points a level
            itself, and the bulk line remap recolors the two dither line inks. See
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :border) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :divider) %> for how levels
            render.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every border slot and remap moves two outputs: the CSS background and the SVG render program that
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.border()</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.divider()</code> export. A slot bound to a
            level carries that level's pattern program. A slot or remap bound to a token exports a flat line in the
            token's stroke color, because a token line has no pattern program of its own.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The chart ramp') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chart-series-ramp($tokens, $span: 6)</code> publishes
            the theme's chart-series ramp. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$tokens</code> is an
            ordered list running from the strongest contrast against the canvas (series 0) toward the canvas tone;
            each slot aliases the token's own paint, so series inherit per-mode dithers and solids for free.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$span</code> is the legible front of the ramp that
            JavaScript spreads series across.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The ramp always publishes 16 slots, so a short list is safe: the slots past your last token are cleared
            rather than left holding the framework grayscale, and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$span</code> never reaches past that token.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Charts read the ramp through <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.series()</code>;
            see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.chart-series-ramp((
    black, yellow-10, yellow-20, yellow-30, yellow-40, yellow-55, yellow-75, yellow
));',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
